    "q-181536": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181536",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,{n}^{2}-5n+6>0}$\n- $B={m\\in Z\\,:\\,{m}^{2}-5m+6<0}$\n- $C={q\\in Q\\,:\\,{q}^{2}-5q+6<0}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14122998/assets/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
