    "q-181667": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181667",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{q\\pi }{2})=0}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
