    "q-181643": {
      "dom_id": "step-q181643",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x-1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x/2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$",
      "worked_solution": "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x-1}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})=-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})=4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})=-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{-2x},-2{e}^{-2x},4{e}^{-2x},-8{e}^{-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
